Who is the greatest?
At that time, the disciples came to Jesus and asked him, "Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?"
Then Jesus called a child to him and placed the child among them,
And He said: "Truly I tell you, unless you change and become like little children, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Therefore, whoever humbles himself like this little child shall be the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
"And whoever welcomes a child like this in my name welcomes me." A warning against stumbling blocks
But whoever causes one of these little believers to stumble because of me, it would be better for him if a millstone were hung around his neck and he were thrown into the sea and drowned!
Woe to the world from these stumbles that are bound to come, but woe to those who cause them!
So if your hand or your foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away from you! It is better to enter eternal life crippled than to have two hands or two feet and be thrown into eternal fire.
And if your eye causes you to sin, pluck it out and cast it away from you! For it is better to enter life with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into the fire of Hell.» The Lost Sheep
And Jesus said: "Beware of despising any of my simple disciples. For I tell you that the angels assigned to protect them always see the face of my Father who is in heaven.
For the Son of Man came to save the lost.
What do you say about a man who has a hundred sheep, and one of them goes astray? Does he not leave the ninety-nine sheep on the mountain to go and find the one that was lost?
I tell you the truth, when he finds it, he will be happier with it than with the ninety-nine sheep that did not go astray.
In the same way, your Father in heaven does not want any one of these simple disciples of mine to perish. Your brother, Saleh
If your brother wrongs you, go to him and speak with him privately. If he listens to you, you have gained your brother.
But if he does not listen to you, take one or two others with you, so that every matter may be established by the testimony of two or three witnesses. #Two or three witnesses. 16:18 See Deuteronomy 19:15.
If they refuse to listen to you, tell it to the church. If they refuse to listen to the church, then you must treat them as you would a pagan or a tax collector.
Truly, I say to you, whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. And whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Truly I tell you, if two of you agree on anything they ask for, it will be done for them by my Father in heaven.
"For where two or three gather in my name, there am I with them." Unlimited forgiveness
Then Peter came to Jesus and said to him, "Lord, how many times shall I forgive my brother who sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Then Jesus said to him, "Not seven times only, but seventy times seven times each time!" #Seventy … times. 22:18 Meaning without limits.
Therefore, the kingdom of heaven can be likened to a king who decided to settle accounts with his servants.
And when he began settling his accounts, a man who owed him a very large sum came to him. #A very large sum. 24:18 Literally: "Ten thousand weights or quintals, which is equivalent to about 300,000 kilograms of the coins used at that time."
And since he did not have anything to repay the debt, the master decided that the debtor, along with his wife, children, and all his possessions, would be sold, and that the price would be used to settle the debt.
Then the servant knelt before the king and pleaded with him, saying: "Have patience with me, and I will pay you all the debt."
So the master took pity on him, canceled his entire debt, and let him go.
And while he was leaving, he found one of his fellow slaves who owed him a small amount. So he grabbed him by the neck and began to choke him, saying: "Settle your debt to me."
So the servant prostrated on his knees before him, pleading and saying: "Have patience with me, and I will pay what I owe."
But he refused that, instead he took him and threw him into prison until he paid all his debt.
And when the other slaves saw what had happened, they were very sad and went to tell their master everything that had happened.
So his master called him and said to him: "O wicked servant, should I not forgive you all the debt you owe because you pleaded with me?"
Shouldn't you have shown mercy to the servant, your companion, just as I have shown mercy to you?
His master became very angry and handed him over to be punished until he paid off all his debt.
"This is how my Heavenly Father will treat you as well, unless each of you forgives your brother from your heart."
Unity in marriage
And after Jesus finished speaking about these matters, he left the region of Galilee and went to the region of Judea beyond the Jordan River.
And large crowds of people followed him, and he healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to Jesus to test him, and said, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any reason?"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read in the Scriptures that from the beginning God 'made them male and female'?" #Created … and female. 4:19 From the Book of Genesis 1:27, 5:2.
Then he said: "Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother and hold fast to his wife, and they shall become one flesh." #leave … one. Genesis 2:24.
And thus they are no longer two, but one. Therefore, no one should separate what God has joined together.
They said to him: "Why then did Moses command that the wife be given a certificate of divorce, #certificate of divorce. 7:19 See Deuteronomy 24:1."
Jesus answered them, "Moses allowed this because of your hard hearts, but it was not this way from the beginning.
Therefore I tell you that anyone who divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, and marries another woman commits adultery.»
So his disciples said to him, "If this is the case between husband and wife, it is better not to marry!"
Then Jesus said to them, "Not everyone can accept this teaching, but only those to whom God gives the ability to do so."
There are men who cannot marry because they were born without the ability to marry. And there are men who cannot marry because people have taken away their ability to marry. And there are men who have chosen not to marry for the sake of the kingdom of heaven. Whoever can accept this teaching, let him accept it.» Jesus welcomes the children
Then they brought children to him so that he might lay his hands on them and pray, but his disciples rebuked them.
Then Jesus said, "Let the children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Then Jesus laid his hands on them and left that place. The obstacle of wealth
A man came to Jesus and asked him, "Teacher, what good deed must I do to have eternal life?"
Jesus answered him, "Why do you ask me about what is good? No one is good except God alone. But if you want to enter into life, keep the commandments."
The man said: "What commandments?" Jesus said: "Do not kill, do not commit adultery, do not steal."
Do not bear false witness, honor your father and your mother.› #Do not kill … and your mother. 19:19 From the Book of Exodus 20: 12-16, and the Book of Deuteronomy 5: 16-20. And ‹love your neighbor #your neighbor. 19:19 By referring to the Gospel of Luke 10: 25-37, we understand that the meaning of neighbor is every person in need of help. As you love yourself.› #love … yourself. 19:19 From the Book of Leviticus 19: 18. »
The young man said to him, "I obey all these commandments, so what do I still lack?"
Jesus said to him, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell all that you have and give to the poor. In this way, you will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard that, he went away sad because he was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "Truly I tell you, it is hard for a rich man to enter the kingdom of heaven.
I tell you again, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God.
When his disciples heard this, they were astonished and said, "Then who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "With man this is impossible, but with God all things are possible."
Then Peter said to him, "See, we have left everything to follow you! What then will we have?"
Then Jesus said to them, "Truly I tell you, when the Son of Man sits on his glorious throne in the new age, you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.
And whoever leaves homes or brothers or sisters or father or mother or children or fields for My sake, he will receive a hundredfold and will inherit eternal life with God forever.»
Many of the first people will become the last people, and many of the last people will become the first people.
Like the workers of generosity
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who owned a vineyard, who went out early in the morning to hire laborers for his vineyard.
He agreed with the workers to pay them one dinar for each day, then sent them to work in his vineyard.
The owner of the vineyard went out around nine o'clock and saw some men standing in the market area doing nothing.
He said to them, "You also go to work in my vineyard, and I will give you the wage you deserve."
So they went to work in the vineyard. "And he went out again around twelve o'clock and also at three o'clock, and hired other workers.
And around five o'clock, he went out again and found others standing in the market area, so he asked them: "Why have you stood all day without working?"
They said to him, "No one has hired us." He said to them, "You also go and work in my vineyard."
At the end of the day, the generous man said to his agent: "Call the workers and pay them their wages, starting with the one who came last, and ending with the one who came first."
Then those who were hired at the fifth hour came, and each of them took a denarius.
Then came those who were hired first, and they thought they would receive more, but each of them also took a denarius.
So they took it and began to grumble about the generous host.
And they say: "Those who were hired last, worked only one hour, and they were paid as much as we were, even though we worked all day under the scorching sun!"
The owner of the vineyard said to one of them: "Did I wrong you, my friend? Did we not agree that you would work for one dinar?"
Take your pay and go. For I want to give the one I hired at the end of the day the same wage I gave you.
Do I not have the right to do what I want with what I own? Or are you jealous because I am good with someone else?
"This is how the first will become last, and the last will become first." Jesus predicts his death and resurrection.
And while Jesus was on his way to the city of Jerusalem, he took the twelve aside and said to them:
Here we are going to the city of Jerusalem where the Son of Man will be handed over to the chief priests and teachers of the law, and they will condemn him to death,
And they hand him over to the unbelievers to mock him, flog him, and crucify him. But on the third day, he will rise from the dead." The greatest is the one who serves.
Then came the mother of my sons Zubaydi with her two sons, and she prostrated to him to ask him for something.
Then Jesus said to her, "What do you want?" She said to him, "Promise me that these two sons of mine will sit in your kingdom, one at your right and the other at your left."
Then Jesus said, "You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup #the cup. 22:20 meaning the cup of pain and suffering. Also in verse 23. that I am going to drink?" They said to him, "Yes, we can."
He said to them, "As for my cup, you will drink it; but as for sitting at my right hand and at my left, I cannot grant it to you unless it is prepared for them by my Father."
When the remaining ten heard this request, they were very angry with the two brothers.
Then Jesus called them to him and said, "You know that the rulers of the Gentiles exercise authority over them, and their leaders exercise power over them.
But this should not be among you; rather, whoever wants to be great among you must be your servant.
And whoever wants to be first among you must be a servant to you.
The Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many." Jesus heals the blind.
And as they were leaving the city of Jericho, large crowds of people followed him.
There were two blind men sitting by the roadside. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us."
They rebuked them and ordered them to be silent, but they raised their voices even more: "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us."
Jesus stopped and called them, saying, "What do you want me to do for you?"
They said, "O Master, open our eyes."
Jesus had compassion on them, touched their eyes, and immediately they saw and followed him.
The king lives
As they approached the city of Jerusalem and came to the village of Bethphage near the Mount of Olives, Jesus sent two of his disciples,
And he said to them, "Go to the village ahead of you. You will find a young donkey tied beside its mother; untie them and bring them to me."
And if one of you says something to the other, say to him: "The Lord needs them, and He will return them soon."
This happened to fulfill what the Prophet said:
Say to the beloved city Zion: #Beloved Zion. 5:21 Literally "the daughter Zion." ‹Behold, your king is coming to you, humble and riding on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a donkey.›
So the two disciples went and did as Jesus had said.
So they brought the young donkey and its mother and put their clothes on them, and Jesus sat on the clothes.
Most people spread their rugs on the road. But others cut branches from the trees and spread them on the road.
And the crowds of people who were walking in front of him and behind him were shouting: "Long live the king! #Long live the king. 9:21 Literally: "Hosanna." And its meaning in Hebrew: "Save now." It is most likely here a shout of praise to God and His anointed king. Repeated in verse 15. Long live the son of David. ‹Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord.› Long live the king on high."
And when Jesus entered the city of Jerusalem, the whole city was shaken. The people were asking, "Who is this man?"
And the crowds that followed him said, "This is the prophet Jesus, from the town of Nazareth, in the region of Galilee." Jesus in the temple
Then Jesus entered the temple courtyard and drove out all who were buying and selling in the temple courtyard. He overturned the tables of the money changers and the seats of those selling doves.
And He said to them: "It is written, 'My house shall be called a house of prayer' #MyHouse … prayer. 13:21 from the book of Isaiah 56:7. But you have made it 'a den of thieves!' #DenOfThieves. 13:21 from the book of Jeremiah 7:11."
Some blind and lame people came to him in the temple courtyard, and he healed them.
The chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the wonderful things he did, and the children shouting in the temple area: "Long live the king! Long live the son of David," and they were very angry and said to him: "Do you not hear what these children are saying?"
Jesus answered, "Yes, but have you not read in the Scriptures: 'Out of the mouths of infants and nursing babies, you have prepared praise'?"
Then Jesus left them and went out of the city to the town of Bethany, and spent the night there. The power of faith
And while Jesus was going early in the morning to the city, he became hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the side of the road, so he went towards it, but found only leaves on it. He said to it, "You will no longer bear fruit." Immediately, the fig tree withered.
When the disciples saw this, they were amazed and asked him, "How did the fig tree wither like this?"
He answered them: "Truly I tell you, if you have faith and do not doubt, you will not only be able to do what I did with the fig tree, but if you say to this mountain, 'Be taken up and thrown into the sea,' your words will come true.
"And whatever you ask for in prayer, you will receive if you believe." Doubting the authority of Jesus
And Jesus went to the temple courtyard, and the chief priests and elders of the people came to him while he was teaching, and said to him, "Tell us by what authority you do these things, and who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered them, "I will also ask you, and you tell me: by what authority do I do these things?"
Where did John's baptism come from? From God or from men?" They began to discuss it among themselves, saying, "If we say it is from God, they will ask us, 'Why then did you not believe him?'"
And if we say that he is from among the people, then we fear the people, because they all consider John a prophet.
Therefore they answered Jesus, saying, "We do not know." Jesus said to them, "Neither will I tell you by what authority I do these things." The parable of the two sons
And Jesus said: "What do you say about the following story: A man had two sons. He went to the first and said to him, 'My son, go work in my vineyard today.'"
The son replied, "I do not want to go." But he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to his other son and asked him the same thing. The son replied, "Yes, sir, I will go." But he did not go.
Which of the two sons did what the father wanted?” They said, “The first.” He said to them, “Truly I tell you, the tax collectors and prostitutes will enter the kingdom of God before you.
Because John the Baptist came to show you the way of righteousness, and you did not believe him, but the tax collectors and prostitutes believed him. And even when you saw what they did, you did not repent and believe him. Like the son of the vineyard owner.
And listen to another parable: There was a man who owned land, so he planted a vineyard, enclosed it with a fence, dug a winepress in it, and built a watchtower. Then he leased it to some farmers and went away.
And when the time came to harvest the grapes, he sent his servants to the farmers to collect his share of the grapes.
But the peasants seized his slaves, beat one of them, killed another, and stoned another.
So the owner sent more slaves than he had sent the first time. The farmers treated them in the same way.
Finally, he sent his son, thinking to himself: "They will respect my son."
But when the farmers saw that this was the owner's son, they conferred among themselves and said: "This is the heir, so let us kill him to seize his inheritance."
So they seized him, threw him out of the vineyard, and killed him.
So what do you think the generous man will do with those farmers when he returns?
So they said to him: "He will punish them in a terrible way because they are wicked, then he will give the vineyard to other farmers who will give him the fruit in the fruit season."
And Jesus said to them, "Have you not read the Scripture: 'The stone the builders rejected has become the cornerstone'? The Lord has done this, and it is marvelous in our eyes."
Therefore I tell you: The kingdom of God will be taken away from you and given to a nation producing the fruit of the kingdom.
"Everyone who falls on this stone will be broken, and anyone on whom the stone falls will be crushed!"
And when the chief priests and Pharisees heard Jesus' parables, they knew he was speaking about them.
So they tried to arrest him, but they were afraid of the people who considered Jesus a prophet.
Like a wedding feast
And Jesus spoke to them again in parables, saying:
The kingdom of heaven is like a king who prepared a wedding banquet for his son.
The king sent his servants to summon those invited to the wedding feast, but they did not want to come.
So the king sent other servants and said to them, "Tell those who are invited, 'The feast is ready. The fattened calves and fattened cattle have been slaughtered. Everything is prepared. Come to the wedding feast.'"
But the invited guests did not care about the matter, and each one went on his way. One went to work in his field, and another to his trade.
As for the others, they seized the king's servants, beat them, and killed them.
Then the king became angry and sent his army, who killed those murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants: "The wedding feast is ready, but those who were invited did not deserve it."
So go to the street corners and invite everyone you find to the wedding feast.
They went out into the streets and gathered everyone they found, whether wicked or righteous, until the banquet hall was filled with guests.
And when the king entered to see the guests, he saw a man there who was not wearing wedding clothes.
So the king said to him: "O friend, how did you enter here without wearing wedding clothes?" But the man remained silent.
So the king said to his servants: "Bind his hands and feet, and cast him out into the darkness, where people weep and gnash their teeth."
"For many are called, but few are chosen." The Pharisees try to trap Jesus.
So the Pharisees went away and gathered together to consult on how they might trap Jesus with something he said.
So they sent their disciples to him along with some from the party of Herod, and said to him: "Teacher, we know that you are truthful and teach the way of God with all sincerity. And that you show no partiality, for you do not regard people according to their status.
So tell us your opinion, is it permissible to pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus knew their evil intent, and said to them, "You hypocrites, why are you trying to trap me?"
"Show me the coin you use." So they brought him a denarius.
He said to them: "Whose drawing and name are these engraved on the dinar?"
They said to him, "They belong to Caesar." He said to them, "Then give to Caesar what belongs to Caesar, and give to God what belongs to God."
When they heard this answer, they were very amazed, and they left him and went on their way. The Sadducees try to trap Jesus.
And on that day, some Sadducees, who say that there is no resurrection, came to him and asked:
"O teacher, Moses said #Moses said. 24:22 See Deuteronomy 25:5, 6. If a man dies and leaves no children, his brother shall marry his widow and have a child who shall be credited to the deceased brother."
There were seven brothers among us. The first married and died. Since he had no children, his brother married his widow.
And that happened to the brother, the second, the third, and even the seventh.
And after they all died, the woman died as well.
So which of the seven will be his wife on the Day of Resurrection? For he married them all.
Jesus answered them, "You are in error because you do not know the Scriptures, nor the power of God."
So understand that in eternal life after the resurrection of the dead, people neither marry nor give their daughters in marriage, but they will be like the angels in heaven.
As for the resurrection of the dead, have you not read what God has said?
"I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob." #I am ... Jacob. 32:22 From the Book of Exodus 3:6. And God is not the God of the dead, but of the living."
When the people heard these words, they were very amazed at his teaching. The Greatest Commandment
And when the Pharisees heard that Jesus had silenced the Sadducees, they gathered together.
A scholar in Islamic law asked him, trying to trap him, and said:
"O teacher, what is the greatest commandment in the law?"
And Jesus said to him: "‹You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your mind,› #Love the Lord ... your mind. Deuteronomy 6:5, 37:22.
This is the first and greatest commandment,
As for the second commandment, it is like the first: "Love your neighbor #your neighbor. 39:22 By referring to the Gospel of Luke 10:25-37, we understand that the meaning of neighbor is every person in need of help. As you love yourself." #Love your neighbor … yourself. 39:22 From the book of Leviticus 19:18.
"The entire Sharia and the books of the prophets relate to these two commandments." Christ, Lord of David
While the Pharisees were gathered around him, Jesus asked them:
"What do you think about the Christ? Whose son is he?" The Pharisees answered him, "He is the son of David."
So he said to them: "Then how did David call him 'Lord' when he said, inspired by the Spirit:
"The Lord said to my Lord: Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies a footstool for your feet."
If David calls the Messiah Lord, then how can he be his son?
No one was able to answer him with anything, and no one dared after that to ask him any more questions.
Jesus criticizes the religious leaders
Then Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples.
He said: "The teachers of the law and the Pharisees have surpassed Moses in interpreting the law."
So keep and practice everything they tell you, but do not do their works. For they say, but do not do according to what they say.
They burden people with difficult tasks to bear, yet they themselves do not wish to exert any effort to follow them.
All the righteous deeds they do, they do to be seen by people. They display their piety by increasing the size of their tassels #their tassels. 5:23 Some Jews used to write certain passages from the Bible and place them in small leather pouches, then tie them with cloth tassels to the forehead or left arm, thus showing the intensity of their religiosity. They also lengthen the fringes of their garments.
They love to sit in the best seats at banquets, and in the front seats in synagogues.
And they like people to greet them with special greetings in the markets, and to call them: "O teacher."
But you are not to be called 'Rabbi,' for you have one Teacher, and you are all brothers.
And do not let anyone on earth call you 'father,' for you have one Father, the Heavenly Father.
And do not let people call you "Lord," for you have one Lord, who is Christ.
The greatest among you should be your servant.
Whoever exalts himself will be humbled by God, and whoever humbles himself will be exalted by God.
"Woe to you, Pharisees and teachers of the law, you hypocrites! For you shut the door of the kingdom of heaven in people's faces. You yourselves do not enter, nor do you allow those who are trying to enter to go in."
"Woe to you, Pharisees, teachers of the law, you hypocrites! Because you devour widows' houses and for a show make lengthy prayers. Therefore, you will receive a harsher punishment."
"Woe to you, Pharisees, and teachers of the law, you hypocrites! Because you travel over land and sea to win a single convert, and when they become one, you make them twice as much a child of hell as yourselves."
Woe to you, blind guides, you who say: "If anyone swears by the temple, he is not bound to keep his oath, but if he swears by the gold in the temple, he is bound to keep it!"
O blind fools! Which is greater: the gold in the temple, or the temple that sanctifies the gold?
And you say: "If someone swears by the altar, he is not bound to keep his oath, but if someone swears by the gift on the altar, he is bound to keep it!"
My uncle! Which is greater: the offering on the altar, or the altar that makes the offering sacred?
For if anyone swears by the altar, he swears by it and by everything on it.
And if someone swears by the temple, he swears by it and by the one who dwells in it.
And if anyone swears by the sky, he is swearing by the Throne of God and by the One who is seated upon it.
"Woe to you, Pharisees, and teachers of the law, you hypocrites! You give a tenth of your spices—mint, dill, and cumin. 23:23 The dill plant was used for its seeds in cooking as a spice, and it also has some medicinal uses. And cumin. But you have neglected the more important matters of the law—justice, mercy, and faithfulness. You should have practiced the latter, without neglecting the former."
O blind guides, you strain out a mosquito from your cup, but you swallow a camel!
"Woe to you, Pharisees, and teachers of the law, you hypocrites! You clean the outside of the cup and dish, but inside you are full of greed and wickedness."
You blind Pharisees, first clean the inside of the cup, so that the outside also becomes clean.
"Woe to you, Pharisees, and teachers of the law, you hypocrites. For you are like whitewashed tombs, which look beautiful on the outside, but inside are full of bones and all kinds of impurity."
You also appear righteous to others, but inside you are full of hypocrisy and wickedness.
"Woe to you, Pharisees, teachers of the law, hypocrites! Because you build tombs for the prophets and decorate the graves of the righteous."
And you say: "If we had lived in the days of our ancestors, we would not have participated in killing the prophets."
And by this, you confirm that you are the descendants of those who killed the prophets,
So complete what your ancestors began.
O snakes and children of vipers! How can you escape the judgment of Hell?
Therefore, I tell you that I will send you prophets, wise men, and teachers. Some of them you will kill, some you will crucify, and others you will flog in your synagogues and persecute from town to town.
Therefore, you will be held accountable for the blood of every innocent person killed on the earth: from the blood of innocent Abel to the blood of Zechariah son of Berechiah, #Abel … Zechariah. 35:23 The first and last who were killed according to the time and text of the Old Testament books. (See Genesis 4:8, and 2 Chronicles 24:20) The one who was killed between the temple and the altar.
I tell you the truth, the punishment for all these crimes will fall on this generation. Jesus warns the people of the city of Jerusalem.
O Jerusalem, O Jerusalem, you who kill the prophets and stone the messengers of God sent to you! I have often longed to gather your children together as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings! But you refused.
Now your home will be left empty and abandoned for you!
For I tell you, you will not see me again until you say, "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord." #Blessed … the Lord. 39:23 From Psalm 118:26. »
Jesus Predicts the Destruction of the Temple
And Jesus left the temple grounds. As he was walking, his disciples came to him because they wanted to see the temple buildings.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do you see all these buildings? Truly I tell you, not one stone will be left on another; they will all be thrown down!"
And while Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives, his disciples came to him privately and said, "Tell us, when will these things happen? And what will be the sign of your coming and the end of the age?"
Jesus answered them, "Be careful that you are not deceived.
Many will come in my name, saying, "I am the Christ," and they will deceive many.
You will hear of wars and revolutions, but you should not be afraid. These things must happen, but they will not be the end of the world.»
For nation will rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom. There will be earthquakes and famines,
But all of these will be the first labor pains.
They will hand you over to be punished, and they will kill some of you. All nations will hate you because of my name.
At that time, many will abandon their faith, and one will hand over another to the authorities, and some will hate one another.
False prophets will appear and deceive many.
And because of the increase of wickedness, the love of many believers will grow cold,
But the one who remains faithful to the end, he will be saved.
The proclamation of the kingdom of God will be announced throughout the world as a testimony to the Gentiles, and then the end will come.
"When you see the 'abomination that causes desolation' #the_abomination_that_causes_desolation. 15:24 See the book of Daniel 9:27, 12:11, as well as Matthew 11:31. Which the prophet Daniel referred to, standing in the holy place – so that the reader may understand these words –"
Then let those who are in Judea flee to the mountains,
And the one on the roof does not come down to take his belongings from the house.
The worker in the field does not return to his house to get his cloak.
How difficult are the conditions of pregnant and nursing women in those days!
But pray that your escape is not in winter or on a Saturday.
For there will be great distress at that time, unequaled from the beginning of the world until now—and never to be equaled again.
And if God had not decreed to shorten those days, no one would have survived. But for the sake of His chosen people, God will shorten those days.
If someone says to you, "Look, the Messiah is here," or "Look, he is there!" do not believe it.
For many false Christs and false prophets will appear and perform great signs and wonders to deceive, if possible, even the elect.
Here I have told you everything before it happened.
Someone might say, "Look, the Messiah is in the wilderness," so do not go there. Or he might say, "Look, he is in one of the rooms," so do not believe it.
For as the lightning comes from the east and shines as far as the west, so will the Son of Man be.
Wherever you find the corpse, you will also find the vultures.
And immediately after the distress that will occur in those days, ‹the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light. The stars will fall from the sky, and the heavenly bodies will be shaken.›
At that time, the sign of the Son of Man will appear in the sky. All the tribes of the earth will mourn, and they will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven with great power and glory.
And His angels will be sent with the blast of a loud trumpet, and they will gather those whom He has chosen from the four corners, from the farthest reaches of the sky to its utmost extent.
Learn from the fig tree. As soon as its branches become tender and its leaves appear, you know that summer is near.
So also, when you see all these things, you will know that the time #time. 33:24 Compare with the Gospel of Luke 21:31. Near at the doors.
I tell you the truth: this generation will not pass away until all these things take place.
Heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will never pass away. Only the Father knows.
But no one knows the day or the hour, not even the angels in heaven, nor the Son, but only the Father.
And as it was in the days of Noah, so it will be when the Son of Man comes.
In the days leading up to the flood, people were eating and drinking, marrying and giving their daughters in marriage until the day Noah entered the ark.
For they did not know what would happen until the flood came and took them away. So it will also be at the coming of the Son of Man.
At that time, two men will be working in a field; one will be taken and the other left.
And two women were grinding grain on a millstone; one was taken and the other left.
So be alert, for you do not know when your Lord will come.
Be sure that if the homeowner knew at what hour of the night the thief intended to come, he would wake up and not let him rob his house.
So be ready yourselves too, because the Son of Man will come at an unexpected moment. The good servant and the evil servant
Who then is the faithful and wise servant whom the master puts in charge of his servants to give them their food at the proper time?
Blessed is the servant who, when his master comes, finds him performing his duty.
I tell you the truth, he will put all his possessions under pledge.
As for the wicked servant, he says to himself: "My master is delayed."
He begins by beating his fellow servants and starts eating and drinking with the drunkards.
The master of that servant will come on a day he does not expect, and at an hour he does not know.
So He punishes him and places him with the hypocrites, where people weep and gnash their teeth.
Like the ten girls
Then the kingdom of heaven will be like ten virgins who took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them are stupid, and five are smart.
So the foolish ones took their lamps, but they did not take any extra oil with them.
As for the smart ones, they took extra oil in their jars along with the lamps.
The groom was late, so all the girls dozed off and snored.
But at midnight, someone shouted: "The bridegroom is coming, go out to meet him."
Then the girls woke up and prepared their lamps.
And the foolish women said to the wise ones: "Give us some of your oil, for our lamps are about to go out."
So the intelligent women answered: "We cannot, it is not enough for us, but go to the sellers to buy oil yourselves."
And while they were going to buy the oil, the groom arrived. The smart ones were ready, so they entered with him to the wedding feast. Then the door was closed.
And finally, the rest of the girls came and said: "O sir, O sir, open the door for us."
But he said: "I tell you the truth, I do not know you!"
Therefore, stay awake, because you do not know the day or the hour when the Son of Man will come. Like the three servants.
The kingdom of heaven is also like a man who was about to travel. He called his servants and entrusted them with all his possessions.
So he gave one of them five bags #five bags. 15:25 Literally "weights or qintars," and the weight equals about 27 to 36 kilograms of gold, silver, or copper. From the money, he gave the second two bags, and the third one bag. He gave each according to his ability. Then he traveled immediately afterward.
So the one who took the five bags immediately began investing them in trade, and earned five more bags.
And the one who took the two bags acted like the first, and earned two more bags.
As for the one who took one bag, he went and dug a hole in the ground and hid his master's money in it.
And after a long time, the master of these slaves returned and began to hold them accountable.
Then the one who took the five bags came and earned five more bags and said: "My lord, you gave me five bags, and these are five more bags I have earned."
So his master said to him: "Well done, good and faithful servant. You have been faithful with a few things; therefore I will put you in charge of many things. Enter and share in your master's joy."
Then came the one who took the two bags, and said: "O my master, you gave me two bags, and these are two other bags I have earned."
So his master said to him: "Well done, good and faithful servant. You have been faithful with a little; I will put you in charge of much. Enter and share in your master's joy."
Then came the one who took one bag, and said: "O master, I know that you are a harsh man, for you reap where you did not sow, and gather where you did not scatter seed.
I was afraid of you, so I went and hid your bag in the ground. So take your money.›
His master answered him: "You are an evil and lazy servant. Since you know that I harvest from crops I did not plant, and reap from fields I did not sow,
So why didn't you deposit my money in the bank, and when I returned, I would take my money with interest?
So take the bag from him and give it to the owner of the ten bags.
For to the one who has, more will be given, and he will have an abundance; but from the one who does not have, even what he has will be taken away.
But that servant who is good for nothing, throw him outside, into the darkness, where there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth. The Son of Man is the judge.
And when the Son of Man comes in his glory with all his angels, he will sit on his glorious throne.
Then all the nations will be gathered before him. And he will separate some of them from others, just as a shepherd separates the sheep from the goats in his flock.
He will place the sheep on his right, and the goats on his left.
Then the king will say to those on his right: "Come, you who are blessed by my Father. Take the kingdom prepared for you since the creation of the world.
For I was hungry and you gave me food. I was thirsty and you gave me drink. I was a stranger and you took me in.
I was naked and you clothed me. I was sick and you took care of me. I was in prison and you visited me.
The righteous will answer him: "Lord, when did we see you hungry and feed you, or thirsty and give you drink?"
And when we saw you a stranger, we took you in, or naked, and we clothed you?
And whenever we saw you sick or imprisoned, we visited you.
The king says: "Truly I tell you, whatever you did for one of the least of my brothers, you did it for me."
Then the king will say to those on his left: "Depart from me, you cursed, into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels.
For I was hungry and you did not feed me. I was thirsty and you did not give me drink.
I was a stranger and you did not take me in. I was naked and you did not clothe me. I was sick and in prison and you did not visit me.
The wicked will answer: "Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty or a stranger or naked or sick or in prison, and did not provide for your needs?"
The king says: "I tell you the truth, when you neglected to do this for one of my weak brothers, you neglected to do it for me."
"And thus the wicked go to eternal punishment, while the righteous go to eternal life."
Jewish leaders are planning to kill Jesus
After Jesus had finished saying this, he said to his disciples:
"You know that the Passover is the day after tomorrow, and the Son of Man will be handed over to the hands of his enemies to be crucified."
The chief priests and the elders of the people had gathered in the palace of the high priest Caiaphas.
And they planned to arrest Jesus by deceit and kill him.
And they were saying: "We should not do this during the feast, to avoid unrest among the people." A woman pours perfume on Jesus.
And while Jesus was in the town of Bethany at the house of Simon the leper,
A woman came to him, carrying precious perfume in a marble bottle, and she poured it on his head while he was eating.
And when his disciples saw that, they became angry and said, "Why this waste?"
It was possible for this perfume to be sold for a large amount of money given to the poor.»
Jesus knew what they were talking about, and said to them, "Why do you trouble this woman? For she has done a wonderful work for me."
The poor you will always have with you, #the poor … always. 11:26 See Deuteronomy 15:11. But I will not always be with you.
I poured the perfume on my body to prepare it for burial.
I tell you the truth: wherever this gospel is preached in the world, what this woman has done will also be told, in memory of her.” Judas Iscariot betrays Jesus
Then one of the twelve, called Judas Iscariot, went to the chief priests,
And he said to them, "What will you give me if I deliver Jesus to you?" So they counted out to him thirty pieces of silver.
From that moment, Judas began looking for a suitable opportunity to hand Jesus over to them. At the Passover table
On the first day of the Feast of Unleavened Bread, the disciples came to him and said, "Where do you want us to prepare the Passover meal for you?"
Then Jesus said, "Go to so-and-so in the village, and tell him: 'The Teacher says: My appointed time is near, and I will celebrate the Passover with my disciples at your house.'"
So the disciples did as Jesus had instructed them, and prepared the Passover meal.
And when evening came, Jesus was reclining at the table with his twelve disciples.
And while they were eating, he said to them, "Truly, I say to you, one of you will betray me."
They were saddened and began to ask him one by one: "Is it I, Lord?"
Jesus answered, "The one who dips his hand with me in the dish is the one who will betray me."
The Son of Man is going according to what is written about him, but woe to that man who betrays the Son of Man. It would have been better for him if he had never been born!
Then Judas, who was about to betray him, asked, "Is it I, Rabbi?" Jesus answered, "You have said so!" The Lord's Supper
And while they were eating, he took bread, blessed God, broke it, and gave it to the disciples, saying: "Take, eat; this is my body."
Then he took a cup of wine, gave thanks, and gave it to them, saying, "Drink from this, all of you."
Because this is my blood, the blood of the covenant that is poured out for many for the forgiveness of their sins.
And I tell you, I will not drink this wine until the day I drink it new in the kingdom of my Father.
Then, they sang some hymns, and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus predicts Peter's denial.
Then Jesus said to them, "You will all lose your faith in me tonight. For it is written: 'I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered.'"
But after I rise from the dead, I will go ahead of you to Galilee.»
Then Peter answered him, "Even if everyone else loses their faith in you, I can never lose my faith in you."
Jesus said to him, "Truly I tell you, this very night, before the rooster crows, you will deny me three times."
Then Peter said to him, "Even if I have to die with you, I will never deny you!" And all the disciples said the same. Jesus prays alone
Then Jesus went with them to a place called Gethsemane, and said to the disciples, "Sit here while I go over there to pray."
And he took Peter and the two sons of Zebedee with him, and he began to feel sorrow and distress.
Then he said to them, "My sorrow is so great that it almost kills me! Stay here and keep watch with me."
And Jesus withdrew from them a little, and bowed his face to the ground and began to pray: "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me #the cup. 39:26 meaning the cup of pain and suffering. Repeated in verse 42. But not as I will, but as you will."
He came to his disciples and found them sleeping, and said to Peter, "Could you not watch with me one hour?"
Stay awake and pray so that you do not fall into temptation. Your spirit is willing, but your body is weak.
He stepped back again to pray, then said: "My Father, if it is not possible for this cup to pass from me, but I must drink it, then let Your will be done."
Then he returned again and found them asleep, for drowsiness had weighed down their eyes.
So he left them and went a third time to pray, saying the same words he had said the first time.
Then he returned to the disciples and said to them, "Are you still sleeping and resting? Behold, the time has come, and the Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of sinners."
Arise, let us go. Here comes the man who betrayed me.» The arrest of Jesus
While he was still speaking, Judas, one of the twelve, appeared, accompanied by a large crowd carrying swords and clubs, sent by the chief priests and the elders of the people.
And the traitor had given them a sign and said: "The one I kiss is the man you are looking for, so seize him."
Now he came close to Jesus and said to him, "Greetings, Teacher!" and kissed him.
Then Jesus said to him, "Friend, do what you came to do." Then they came and seized Jesus and arrested him.
Then one of those who were with Jesus reached out his hand, drew his sword, and struck the servant of the high priest, cutting off his ear.
Then Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back in its place, for all who take the sword will perish by the sword."
Do you not realize that I can call on the Father, and He will send me more than twelve legions of angels immediately?
But if you do, how will the books that declared these things would happen this way come true?
And at that moment Jesus said to the crowds: "Did you come out against me with swords and clubs as you would against a criminal? I was daily with you in the temple teaching, and you did not seize me!
But this happened to fulfill what the prophets had written." Then all the disciples deserted him and fled! Jesus before the Jewish leaders
Then those who had arrested him took him to the house of Caiaphas the high priest, where the teachers of the law and the elders had assembled.
But Peter followed him from a distance and entered the courtyard of the high priest's house. He went in and sat with the guards to see what would happen in the end.
The chief priests and all the members of the Jewish council were looking for false testimony against Jesus in order to put him to death.
But they did not find, although many false witnesses came forward and told lies about him. Finally, two men came forward,
And they said: "This man #this man. 61:26, meaning Jesus, for his enemies avoided pronouncing his name! He said: 'I can destroy the temple of God and rebuild it in three days.'"
Then the high priest stood up and said, "Aren't you going to defend yourself against all the accusations these two men are making against you?"
But Jesus remained silent. The high priest said to him, "I adjure you by the living God to tell us if you are the Christ, the Son of God."
Jesus answered him, "Yes, I am he, as you said. And I tell you: from now on, you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of the throne of God, and coming on the clouds of heaven."
Then the chief priest tore his clothes and said, "I have insulted God; what further need is there for witnesses? You have now heard his insult to God."
"So what is your judgment?" They answered, "He deserves to die."
Then they spat in his face, struck him with their fists, and slapped him.
And they were saying to him, "Prophesy to us, O Christ, who is it that struck you?" Peter denies Jesus
Meanwhile, Peter was sitting in the courtyard outside. Then the servant girl of the high priest came to him and said, "You also were with Jesus the Galilean."
But Peter denied this in front of everyone and said, "I don't understand what you are saying!"
Then he went out to the gate of the courtyard, and another servant girl said to those who were there, "This man was with Jesus the Nazarene."
But Peter denied it again, and swore, saying, "I do not know this man."
After a while, those standing there came to him and said: "You really are one of them, for your accent reveals that you are Galilean."
Then he began to curse #curse. 74:26 That is, he swore by himself with a curse if he was lying! And he swore and said: "I do not know this man." And immediately the rooster crowed.
Then Peter remembered the word Jesus had said to him: "You will deny me three times before the rooster crows," and he went out and wept bitterly.
Jesus before Governor Pilate
And on the next morning, the chief priests and the elders of the people gathered and conspired to kill Jesus.
They seized him, led him away, and handed him over to the governor Pilate. Judas kills himself.
When Judas, who betrayed Jesus, saw that they had decided to sentence Jesus to death, he regretted what he had done. So he returned the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and elders,
And he said to them, "I have made a mistake by handing over an innocent person to be killed." They said to him, "What does this have to do with us? Handle this matter yourself."
Then Judas threw the pieces of money into the temple and left, and went and hanged himself.
So the chief priests took the pieces of silver and said, "It is not lawful to put this money into the temple treasury, because it is the price of blood."
They decided to buy the potter's field with it to be a cemetery for strangers.
For this reason, the field is known as the "Field of Blood" to this day.
And thus was fulfilled what God said through the prophet Jeremiah: "They took the thirty pieces of silver, the price at which the sons of Israel agreed to pay."
"And they bought the potter's field with it, as the Lord commanded me." #They took … the Lord. 10:27 See the book of Zechariah 11:12-13, and the book of Jeremiah 32:6-9. Jesus before Pilate
And Jesus stood before the governor, and he asked him, "Are you the King of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "It is as you say."
And when the chief priests and elders were accusing him, he did not say a word.
Then Pilate asked him, "Do you not hear all these many accusations they are bringing against you?"
But Jesus did not give Pilate a response to any of the accusations against him. Pilate was amazed at his silence. Pilate failed to release Jesus.
The governor was accustomed during Easter to release a prisoner chosen by the people.
And there was a famous prisoner named Barabbas. #Barabbas. 16:27 or "Jesus Barabbas" as in some Greek manuscripts.
When the people gathered, Pilate said to them, "Whom do you want me to release to you: Jesus called the Christ, or Barabbas?"
Pilate knew that they had handed Jesus over to him out of envy.
And while Pilate was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent him a message saying: "Do not harm this innocent man, for I have been troubled all night because of a dream about him."
But the chief priests and the elders persuaded the crowd to ask for the release of Barabbas and the killing of Jesus.
So the governor said, "Which one do you want me to release for you?" They said, "Barabbas."
Then Pilate asked them, "What shall I do with Jesus called the Christ?" They all answered, "Let him be crucified."
Then Pilate said to them, "Why? What crime has he committed?" But they shouted even more, "Crucify him."
When Pilate saw that there was no benefit in his attempt, and that chaos had begun, he took some water and washed his hands before the crowd, saying: "I am not responsible for the death of this man; it is your responsibility."
So all the people said, "His blood be on us and on our children."
Then Pilate released Barabbas to them, and ordered Jesus to be flogged, and handed him over to be crucified. The soldiers mock Jesus.
Then the governor's soldiers led Jesus to the governor's palace, and they gathered the guard battalion against him,
So they took off his clothes and put on him a robe of crimson color. #They put on him … the color. 28:27 Or purple, and that was to mock him, for this is the color of a king's robe.
And they twisted a crown of thorns for him and placed it on his head, and put a reed in his right hand, and they knelt before him mocking, saying: "Long live the King of the Jews!"
Then they spat on him, took the cane from his hand, and began hitting him on the head.
And when they finished mocking him, they took off his robe, put his own clothes on him, and led him out to crucify him. Jesus on the cross
When they went out, they found a man from the city of Cyrene named Simon, and they forced him to carry the cross.
And when they arrived at a place known as "Golgotha," meaning "the place of the skull,"
They gave Jesus wine mixed with a bitter substance to drink. When he tasted it, he refused to drink.
And when they crucified Jesus, they divided his clothes among them, and cast lots for them.
Then they sat there guarding him.
And they hung above his head a sign that read: "This is Jesus, King of the Jews," as his charge.
And two criminals were crucified with Jesus, one on his right and the other on his left.
The Maronites used to insult him and shake their heads.
And they say, "You who will destroy the temple and build it in three days, if you are the Son of God, save yourself, and come down from the cross!"
And the chief priests, the teachers of the law, and the elders also mocked him and said:
He saves others, but he cannot save himself! He is the King of Israel! Let him come down from the cross now, and we will believe in him!
He placed his trust in God; let God save him now if He truly wills. Did he not say, "I am the Son of God"?
And the two criminals crucified with him were also insulting him with similar words. The death of Jesus
From twelve noon, darkness covered the entire land until three in the afternoon.
And about the third hour, Jesus cried out in a loud voice and said: "Eli, Eli, lema sabachthani?" #Eli … sabachthani. 46:27 From Psalm 22:1. That is: "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?"
And when some of those standing there heard it, they said, "He is calling Elijah!" #Calling Elijah. 47:27 The word "Eli" in Hebrew and "Elo" in Aramaic resembles the name "Elijah," which is the name of a well-known prophet who lived around 850 BC.
Then one of the bystanders quickly took a sponge, dipped it in vinegar, placed it on a long stick, and offered it to him to drink.
As for the others, they were saying: "Let's wait and see if Elijah will come to save him!"
Then Jesus cried out again with a loud voice and gave up his spirit.
Then the curtain of the temple #temple curtain. 51:27 The curtain that separated the "Holy of Holies" from the rest of the Jewish temple. The Holy of Holies represented the divine presence. It was torn in two from top to bottom, the earth shook, and the rocks split,
And the graves were opened, and many bodies of the holy believers who had died were raised.
And after Jesus rose, those bodies came out of their graves, entered the holy city, and appeared to many.
As for the Roman officer, #Roman officer. 54:27 literally "centurion." And the guards who were guarding Jesus' body, when they saw the earthquake and the other events, were very frightened and said, "Truly this was the Son of God!"
And there were women standing and watching from a distance, who had followed Jesus from Galilee to serve him.
Among them are Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Joseph, and also the mother of the sons of Zebedee. #sons of Zebedee. 56:27 James and John. The burial of Jesus
And when evening came, a rich man named Joseph came to the city of Jerusalem, and he was from the town of Ramah. He was also a disciple of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked him for the body of Jesus. So Pilate ordered that the body be given to him.
So Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean linen cloth,
Then he placed him in his new grave, which he had dug in the rock, then rolled a huge stone over the entrance of the grave and left.
Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting opposite the tomb. Guarding the tomb of Jesus
And the next day, after Friday was over, the chief priests and the Pharisees gathered with Pilate,
And they said to him: "Lord, we remember that this deceiver said before he died: 'I will rise from the dead after three days.'"
He issued an order to guard the tomb until the third day, so that his disciples would not come and steal the body and then tell the people: "He has risen from the dead." This deception would be worse than the first deception.
Then Pilate said to them, "Take guards of soldiers, and go, and make sure of everything by your own knowledge."
So they went and secured the grave. They placed a seal on the stone, and stationed guards of soldiers over it.
The Resurrection of Jesus
And after the end of Saturday, on the first day of the week, Mary Magdalene and the other Mary came to the tomb.
At that moment, a strong earthquake occurred, because the angel of the Lord came down from heaven, went to the tomb, rolled the stone away from the door, and then sat on it.
And his appearance was shining like lightning, and his clothes were white like snow.
The guards were very afraid of him and became like the dead.
And the angel said to the two women: "Do not be afraid, I know that you are looking for Jesus who was crucified.
He is not here; he has risen from the dead as he said before. Come and see the place where he lay,
Then go quickly to his disciples and say to them: He has risen from the dead, and he will go ahead of you to Galilee, and you will see him there. Behold, I have told you.»
Then the two women quickly left the tomb, their fear mingled with great joy, and they ran to tell Jesus' disciples what had happened.
And suddenly Jesus met them and said, "Peace." So they came to him, took hold of his feet, and worshiped him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid; go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee, and there they will see me." The false report
And while the two women were on their way, some guards went to the city and told the chief priests everything that had happened.
Then the chief priests gathered with the elders and consulted together, and they gave the soldiers a large sum of money,
And they said to them, "Spread among the people that Jesus' disciples came at night and stole his body while you were asleep."
And if this news reaches the governor, we will convince him and keep you safe.»
So the soldiers took the money and did as they were told. And thus this story spread among the Jews to this day. Jesus speaks to his disciples
And the eleven disciples went to Galilee, to the mountain that Jesus had told them to go to.
And when they saw him, they prostrated to him, although some of them had doubts.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
Go therefore, and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit,
And teach them to obey everything I have commanded you. And remember that I will be with you always, to the very end of the age.”
John the Baptist
This is the beginning of the good news about Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
As it is written in the book of the prophet Isaiah: "Behold, I send my messenger before you, who will prepare your way."
A human voice calls out in the wilderness, saying: "Prepare the way for the Lord. Make straight paths for Him."
John the Baptist came baptizing in the wilderness, urging people to be baptized as a sign of their repentance for the forgiveness of sins.
And all the inhabitants of the villages of the Judea region and the city of Jerusalem went out to him. He baptized them in the Jordan River after they confessed their sins.
His clothes were made of camel hair, with a leather belt around his waist, and he ate locusts and wild honey.
And he used to announce and say: "A man greater than me will come after me, and I am not worthy to bend down and untie his shoe strap."
"I baptize you with water, but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." Baptism of Jesus
In those days, Jesus came from the town of Nazareth in the region of Galilee, and was baptized by John in the Jordan River.
And at the moment he came out of the water, he saw the sky open, and he saw the Holy Spirit descending upon him in the form of a dove.
A voice came from heaven: "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased." The experience of Jesus
And the Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness alone.
And he remained there for forty days facing the devil's temptations. He was with the wild animals, and the angels served him. Jesus chooses some of his disciples.
After John was arrested, Jesus came to the region of Galilee and began to proclaim the good news of God.
And he says: "The time has come, and the kingdom of God is near; repent and believe in this good news."
And while he was walking on the shore of the Sea of Galilee, he saw Simon and his brother Andrew casting the net into the lake, for they were fishermen.
Then Jesus said to them, "Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men."
They immediately left their nets and followed him.
Then he walked a little, and saw Jacob son of Zebedee and his brother John in their boat preparing the nets.
Then Jesus called them, and they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the workers and followed him. Jesus casts out an evil spirit.
Then they went to Capernaum, and Jesus entered the synagogue on the Sabbath and began to teach.
They were amazed at his teaching, because he taught them as one who has authority, and not like the teachers of the law.
And there was a man in the synagogue who had an unclean spirit. And the spirit cried out:
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are, you are the Holy One of God.
Jesus rebuked him and said, "Be silent, and come out of him!"
Then the impure spirit entered the man in a convulsive fit, then he cried out loudly and came out of him.
Everyone was astonished and began saying to one another, "What is this new teaching? For he commands even unclean spirits with authority, and they obey him."
The news about him quickly spread throughout the entire region of Galilee. Jesus heals many.
Then they left the complex and went with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
And Simon's mother-in-law was in bed with a fever. They told Jesus about her,
He approached her, took her hand, and sat her down. The fever left her, and she began to serve them.
And that evening, at sunset, they brought to him many patients and those possessed by evil spirits.
The entire town's residents gathered at the door of the house.
Jesus healed many who were suffering from various diseases and cast out many demons. But Jesus did not allow the spirits to speak because they knew who he was. Preparation for proclaiming the gospel.
Early in the morning before sunrise, Jesus went out alone and went to a solitary place to pray.
So Simon and those with him went out to look for him.
And when they found him, they said to him, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered them, "Let us go to the neighboring villages so that I may also preach there, for this is why I came."
He went throughout the region of Galilee, preaching in the synagogues of the Jews and casting out evil spirits. Jesus heals a leper.
A man with leprosy came to Jesus, knelt down, and begged him, saying, "If you are willing, you can make me clean."
Jesus was moved with compassion, stretched out His hand, touched him, and said, "Yes, I will; be clean."
The leprosy was removed from the man, and he became clean.
Then Jesus warned him sternly before sending him away.
And he said to him: "Be careful not to tell anyone what happened to you, but go and show yourself to the priest, #go … to the priest. 44:1 The priest was the one who decided according to the law when the leper was considered clean. And offer a sacrifice for your cleansing as Moses commanded, #as Moses commanded. 44:1 See Leviticus 14:1-32. So that people may know that you have been healed."
But the man went out and began to spread the news of his healing everywhere, so it became difficult for Jesus to enter any city openly; rather, he stayed in remote places, and people came to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a paralyzed man
After several days, Jesus returned to Capernaum, and news of his return spread.
A large crowd gathered so that there was no room left, not even outside the door. And Jesus was speaking to the people with the word of God.
So they came to him with a paralyzed man carried by four men.
But they could not bring him to Jesus because of the crowd. So they made an opening in the roof above where Jesus was, and when they had made an opening, they lowered the mat the paralyzed man was lying on.
When Jesus saw their faith, he said to the paralyzed man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some of the teachers of Sharia were sitting there, and they began to think to themselves:
"Why does this man speak in this way? He is insulting God with his words! Who other than God alone can forgive sins?"
Jesus knew their thoughts and said to them, "Why are you thinking these things in your hearts?"
Which of the two is easier: to say to the paralyzed, "Your sins are forgiven," or to say, "Get up, take your bed, and walk"?
But I will show you that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." And he said to the paralyzed man:
"I am telling you, get up, make your bed, and go to your house!"
He got up, immediately took up his bed, and walked in full view of everyone. Everyone was amazed and praised God, saying, "We have never seen anything like this before!" Levi (Matthew) follows Jesus.
And Jesus returned again to the lake. He was teaching the crowds that followed him there.
And as he was walking, he saw a Levite sitting at the tax collection place. He said to him, "Follow me!" So he got up and followed him.
While Jesus was sitting at the house of Levi eating dinner, many tax collectors and sinners were eating with him and his disciples. For many were there when Jesus called Levi, and they followed Jesus.
When the Pharisees and the teachers of the law saw him eating with tax collectors and sinners, they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
When Jesus heard them, he said to them, "It is not the healthy who need a doctor, but the sick. I have not come to call the righteous, but sinners." A question about fasting
Now the time for fasting came, and the disciples of John and the Pharisees were fasting. Some people came to Jesus and asked him, "Why do the disciples of John and the Pharisees fast, but your disciples do not fast?"
Then Jesus said to them, "Can the wedding guests fast while the bridegroom is with them? As long as the bridegroom is with them, they cannot fast.
But the time will come when the groom will be taken from them, and then they will fast.
No one patches an old garment with a piece of new cloth, because the new cloth will shrink and tear the old garment, making the hole worse.
No one puts new wine into old wineskins, because the new wine will burst the wineskins, and the wine will be spilled and the wineskins ruined. Therefore, new wine is put into new wineskins." Jesus: Lord of the Sabbath
And on one of the Saturdays, Jesus was passing through some fields, and his disciples began to pick the ears of grain as they walked with him.
Then the Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look! Your disciples are doing what is not lawful to do on the Sabbath!"
He said to them: "Have you never read in the book what David did when he was in need and hungry, he and those with him?"
He entered the house of God during the time of the priest Abiathar, and ate the consecrated bread that was offered to God, and also gave some to those who were with him. Although no one is allowed to eat that bread except the priests." #26:2 See the book of 1 Samuel 21:1-6.
Then Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for the benefit of man, not man for the Sabbath."
"And so the Son of Man is also Lord of the Sabbath."
Jesus heals on the Sabbath day
And Jesus went again to the synagogue, and there was a man whose hand was withered.
And some people closely watched him to see if he would heal him, to find a reason to accuse him.
So he said to the man with the paralyzed hand, "Get up and come!"
Then he said to them: "Is it permissible to do good or harm on the Sabbath? Is it permissible to save a human life or to kill it?" They remained silent.
Jesus looked around at them with anger and was saddened by the hardness of their hearts. Then he said to the man, "Stretch out your hand," and he stretched it out, and it was restored.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the followers of Herod on how to kill Jesus. Many followed Jesus.
Jesus went with his disciples to the Sea of Galilee, and a large crowd from Galilee and also from Judea followed them.
From Jerusalem, Edom, and east of the Jordan River, and the areas surrounding Tyre and Sidon, there was a great multitude. They all came to him because of what they had heard about his deeds.
Jesus asked his disciples to prepare a boat for him so that the crowds would not press upon him.
Since Jesus had healed many, everyone who was suffering from an illness tried to reach him to touch him.
And the unclean spirits would throw themselves down before him and cry out, "You are the Son of God!"
He strongly warns her not to reveal who he is. The Choice of the Twelve
Then Jesus went up on the mountain and called to him those he wanted, and they went with him.
And Jesus chose twelve men and named them apostles, to be with him, and to send them to different places,
And He gives them authority to cast out evil spirits.
And he is: Simon, whom he named Peter,
James son of Zebedee and his brother John, whom Jesus called "Boanerges" – meaning "sons of thunder,"
Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, James son of Alphaeus, Simon the Zealot, #the Zealot. 18:3 From an Aramaic word meaning "the zealous." That is, belonging to a Jewish political party that resisted Roman rule, called the "Zealots."
And Judas Iscariot who betrayed him. The power of Jesus from God and Jesus returned home.
And the people gathered around him and his disciples again so much that they did not find time to eat.
And when Jesus' family heard about his coming, they came to take him away, because people were saying that he was crazy!
As for the teachers of Sharia who came from Jerusalem, they used to say: "Indeed, in it is Beelzebub, #Beelzebub. 22:3 One of the names of the devil. And he casts out evil spirits by the power of the prince of the evil spirits."
Then Jesus called them and began to speak to them in parables, saying: "How can Satan cast out a demon?"
For if a kingdom is divided and its people fight against each other, it will not endure.
And if a house is divided against itself, it will not stand.
